Largest in Array
Solved
Difficulty: BasicAccuracy: 67.48%Submissions: 639K+Points: 1Average Time: 20m
Given an array arr[]. The task is to find the largest element and return it.

Examples:

Input: arr[] = [1, 8, 7, 56, 90]
Output: 90
Explanation: The largest element of the given array is 90.


class Solution {
    public int largest(List<int> arr) {
        // code here.
        int maxValue = int.MinValue;
        foreach(int num in arr){
            if(maxValue<num){
                maxValue = num;
            }
        }
        
        return maxValue;
        
    }
}
Overall Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity = O(1)

Approach 2: Sort the Array
arr.Sort();

return arr[arr.Count - 1];
Complexity
Time: O(n log n)
Space: Depends on the sorting algorithm

❌ Worse than the linear scan because sorting does extra work when you only need the maximum.

Approach 3: LINQ
return arr.Max();
Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(1)

This is concise, but in an interview, it's often better to implement the logic yourself unless the interviewer explicitly allows library methods.

Interview Follow-up

An interviewer might ask:

Can you do it in O(log n)?

Answer
For an unsorted array: No. You must inspect every element, so O(n) is the best possible.
For a sorted array: Yes. The largest element is at the end.
return arr[arr.Count - 1];

This access is O(1) because no traversal is needed.


First and Second Smallests
